2051: that script over the submitted files and states the boundary that no Lean build,
2052: publication-daemon run, or Rule110 dynamic artifact rerun is being certified.
2053: The negative fixture log
2054: \path{review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example_run.md} records the command
2055: \path{python review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py --negative-example review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example.json}
2056: and the expected rejection of a route row whose evidence level is raised to
2057: command-run without the required command-run tuple.
2058: Consequently the executable artifact proves exactly the schema-level instance of
2059: $V$ claimed here.  By the schema-relative finite-record exactness of Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, this is also
2060: an instance of the paper's finite record-acceptance semantics: the executable
2061: record accepts exactly the current-package certificates and rejects the
2062: recorded command-run escalation example; hence it supplies no stronger evidence
2063: level or omitted gate semantics.
2064: 
2065: Finally, \path{review_bundle/submission_interface_map.json} has rows
2066: \path{sim1}--\path{sim4}.  The verifier now rejects a mutation of any row's
2067: primary claim text, certificate list, row-or-manifest surface list, or evidence
2068: level, so the JSON file is an executable finite mirror of the interface table at
2069: that declared field level.  The file
2070: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json} lists the concrete primary-note
2071: assertions as rows \path{pc1}--\path{pc12}; every load-bearing row names a
2072: certificate, row, manifest surface, or bounded-context status and declares an
2073: evidence level, and the verifier locks each row's identifier, source location,
2074: status class, and evidence level.  Thus the primary note has a complete finite
2075: claim inventory at the declared review fields, and no additional primary
2076: load-bearing assertion is licensed by the machine-readable interface.  The proof
2077: does not assert that the script decides semantic paraphrase equality or discovers
2078: undeclared paths; those remain the manual audit obligations represented by the
2079: table rows and by Proposition~\ref{prop:primary-inventory-freshness-criterion}.\end{proof}
2080: 
2081: \begin{theorem}[Executable record non-escalation]
2082: \label{thm:executable-record-non-escalation}
2083: Let $F$ be the finite field projection read by
2084: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}: manifest-field presence,
2085: entries $a_1$--$a_6$, claim-kind membership, evidence-level membership,
2086: assigned-gate coverage, pass-record fields, evidence-level agreement,
2087: discovery-field discipline, path-verified boundary status, row locks for
2088: \path{sim1}--\path{sim4}, and row-class locks for \path{pc1}--\path{pc12}.  Assuming
2089: the exact-or-archive-equivalent review-bundle support surface of
2090: Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition}, among all
2091: assertions about the current review bundle that are functions only of this
2092: projection, Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records} proves the
2093: maximal executable claim: the six current-package certificates satisfy the finite
2094: record-acceptance relation of Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} at the
2095: declared path-verified fields under the input--predicate--output contract of
2096: Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract}, and no accepted row carries a
2097: command-run boundary.  Thus the bundled Python script is an implementation
2098: witness for the finite schema, not a separately formalized verifier-language
2099: semantics.  In particular, the executable verifier cannot certify any strictly
2100: stronger assertion whose truth depends on prose-to-semantics equality,
2101: independent extraction of claims from the manuscript, completeness of paths not
2102: declared in the records, or the external semantics of a Lean development,
2103: publication daemon, artifact suite, evaluator, or venue state.
2104: \end{theorem}
2105: 
2106: \begin{proof}
2107: By the proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}, the
2108: script reads exactly the finite projection $F$ listed in the statement and rejects
2109: mutations of the locked fields.  Hence every executable assertion made by that
2110: script is constant on the equivalence classes of review bundles with the same
2111: projection $F$.  On this quotient, the six ledger entries, their kind sets, their
2112: evidence levels, their assigned gates, their pass-record fields, their discovery
2113: boundaries, and their path-verified non-command boundaries are all fixed.  By
2114: Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract}, the executable checker has no
2115: other mathematical output channel.  The schema-relative finite-record acceptance exactness
2116: theorem then identifies precisely what this fixed data entails: acceptance of
2117: those six certificates in the finite relation
2118: $\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}$ and rejection of stronger readings that add kinds, raise
2119: the evidence level, omit route or discovery fields, omit pass records, or assert
2120: command-run evidence without the required command tuple.
2121: 
2122: Now consider any proposed stronger assertion of one of the four types named in
2123: the statement.  Its truth is not determined by $F$.  Prose-to-semantics equality
2124: depends on interpreting manuscript language against mathematical meanings, while
2125: $F$ contains only row identifiers, selected text locks, classes, locations,
2126: certificate lists, surfaces, and evidence levels.  Independent extraction depends
2127: on an extraction procedure over the manuscript, while $F$ contains the submitted
2128: inventory rather than the result of an independently specified extractor.
2129: Completeness of undeclared paths depends on paths outside the finite surfaces
2130: read by the verifier.  External artifact semantics depends on running or
2131: semantically validating objects whose command, source state, environment, exit
2132: code, and log path are not present in the path-verified rows.  Therefore two
2133: packages can agree on $F$ while differing on such a stronger assertion.  Since a
2134: function of $F$ has the same value on both packages, no executable claim proved by
2135: this verifier can certify that stronger assertion.  The maximal executable
2136: record-level claim is exactly the one stated in
2137: Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}.\end{proof}
2138: 
2139: \begin{theorem}[Executable certificate-construction invariant]
2140: \label{thm:executable-certificate-construction-invariant}
2141: For the submitted review bundle, let $G$ be the union of the finite fields read by
2142: the two executable verifiers
2143: \path{review_bundle/verify_source_interface_record.py} and
2144: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}.  Thus $G$ contains the ten
2145: source-interface table triples, the JSON source-interface records, the pinned
2146: source snapshot path, digest, line count, declared source commit and line ranges,
2147: the six current-package pass-record entries $a_1,\ldots,a_6$, the submission-map
2148: rows \path{sim1}--\path{sim4}, the primary-inventory row locks
2149: \path{pc1}--\path{pc12}, and the two recorded successful verifier logs.  On the
2150: equivalence class of packages with the same projection $G$, the publication-safety
2151: interface has a unique executable finite certificate construction: the imported
2152: discovery-gate interface is the sixteen-row checked source interface of
2153: Proposition~\ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency}, the accepted
2154: current-package certificates are exactly the six records of
2155: Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}, and any stronger
2156: reading that changes the source interface, adds an accepted row, raises the
2157: evidence level, adds a command-run assertion, or asserts external semantic or
2158: venue correctness is not determined by $G$ and is rejected at the finite-record
2159: boundary unless new certificate fields and verifier records are supplied.  This
2160: is a finite-record construction invariant: the scripts witness the listed
2161: predicate checks on bundled JSON and table fields, and no claim is made here that
2162: Python itself has been given a separate formal operational semantics.
2163: \end{theorem}
2164: 
2165: \begin{proof}
2166: The first executable verifier reads only the manuscript source-interface table,
2167: \path{source_interface_record.json}, the \path{pinned_source_snapshot} object of
2168: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json}, and the bundled source snapshot.
2169: Proposition~\ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency} proves that these
2170: fields agree on the ordered sixteen triples, the original path, the source commit, the
2171: sixteen line ranges, the snapshot digest, the snapshot line count, and the containment
2172: of each imported label in its declared range.  Hence every package with the same
2173: projection $G$ has the same executable imported discovery-gate interface, and no
2174: table-only, JSON-only, manifest-only, stale-digest, stale-line-count, or
2175: out-of-range imported row can be accepted inside that projection.
2176: 
2177: The second executable verifier reads the manifest fields, the certificate schema,
2178: the six pass-record entries, the submission-interface map, and the primary-claim
2179: inventory.  Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records} proves
2180: that these fields instantiate the finite verifier $V$ for exactly rows
2181: $a_1,\ldots,a_6$ at the path-verified level, with the displayed gate coverage,
2182: discovery-field discipline, row locks, and non-command boundary.  Therefore every
2183: package with the same projection $G$ has the same six accepted current-package
2184: certificates and the same absence of command-run evidence in those certificates.
2185: The input--predicate--output contract,
2186: Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract}, says that these are the only
2187: positive outputs available to the certificate checker; the finite-record
2188: exactness theorem,
2189: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, identifies these accepted records
2190: with the record-acceptance relation and rejects stronger readings that require
2191: additional claim kinds, evidence surfaces, promotion fields, discovery fields,
2192: pass rows, or command-run tuples.
2193: 
2194: Combining the two paragraphs gives the stated uniqueness on the $G$-projection:
2195: the discovery component of any accepted current-package claim is forced through
2196: the same checked ten-row source interface, and the publication component is forced
2197: through the same six checked certificate rows.  Any proposed stronger assertion
2198: listed in the statement either changes one of the finite fields of $G$, in which
2199: case it is not a package in the same equivalence class, or leaves $G$ unchanged,
2200: in which case the two verifiers receive identical inputs and can return only the
2201: same finite-record conclusions.  Such an unchanged projection contains no
2202: coordinate for external theorem truth, proof-assistant correctness, unlogged
2203: daemon execution, artifact semantic validity, or venue acceptance.  Those claims
2204: therefore require an enlarged certificate and additional executable records, and
2205: without them they are outside the accepted finite-record interface.\end{proof}
2206: 
2207: \begin{proposition}[Deterministic claim-kind dispatch]
2208: \label{prop:claim-kind-dispatch}
2209: For every load-bearing manuscript claim whose kind lies in the seven-kind domain
2210: above, $\Gamma$ assigns a unique finite set of deterministic gates.  In a
2211: pipeline that is record-gate-sound for acceptance, a mixed load-bearing claim may be
2212: used as accepted work only if its acceptance entry records passage through every
2213: gate in the union assigned to its component kinds.
2214: \end{proposition}
2215: 
2216: \begin{proof}
2217: The seven atomic clauses in the definition of $\Gamma$ assign one explicitly named
2218: finite gate set to each atomic claim kind.  Hence an atomic claim has a unique
2219: assigned gate set.  For a mixed claim, the component-kind set is finite because
2220: it is a subset of the seven-kind domain; taking the union of the corresponding
2221: finite gate sets is deterministic and finite.  Record-gate-soundness for acceptance
2222: requires the acceptance entry of a load-bearing claim to record passage through
2223: every gate assigned by $\Gamma$.  If a component gate of a mixed claim were
2224: omitted, then the projection of the claim to that component kind would lack its
2225: assigned pass record.  Such an entry would not be record-gate-sound.  Therefore accepted
2226: mixed claims in a record-gate-sound pipeline must pass every gate in the union.\end{proof}
2227: 
2228: \begin{theorem}[Mixed-claim projection obstruction]
2229: \label{thm:mixed-claim-projection-obstruction}
2230: Let $c$ be a load-bearing mixed claim with nonempty component-kind set
2231: $K\subseteq\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)$ and submitted candidate certificate
2232: $\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$.  For a nonempty subset $J\subseteq K$, write
2233: \[
2234: G_J=\bigcup_{k\in J}\Gamma(k)
2235: \]
2236: and let $c_J$ denote the projected reading of $c$ that keeps exactly the
2237: claim-kind components in $J$ and keeps the same declared evidence level,
2238: evidence surface, promotion boundary, and command boundary, except that the
2239: discovery field is required precisely when discovery lies in $J$.  Then the
2240: following are equivalent for the projected reading $c_J$:
2241: \begin{enumerate}
2242: \item the finite verifier accepts the projected certificate
2243: $\mathcal C_J=(J,\ell,E,H,R|_{G_J},D_J,B)$, where $R|_{G_J}$ is the restriction of
2244: $R$ to rows whose gate name lies in $G_J$ and $D_J$ is $D$ exactly when discovery
2245: lies in $J$ and is absent otherwise;
2246: \item every gate $g\in G_J$ has in $R$ a pass record with the five required
2247: fields and with an allowed evidence pointer, the retained boundary fields $E$,
2248: $H$, $D_J$, and $B$ satisfy the corresponding schema clauses at level $\ell$, and
2249: $J\subseteq\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)$.
2250: \end{enumerate}
2251: Consequently, if an acceptance entry for $c$ records passage only through a
2252: proper gate subset $Q\subset\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$, then every component
2253: subset $J$ with $\neg(G_J\subseteq Q)$ has an inclusion-minimal finite bad subrecord,
2254: namely a missing pass record for some gate in $G_J\setminus Q$.  In particular,
2255: no passage through route, submission, scheduler, artifact, traceability,
2256: formal-source, or discovery gates can certify a mixed claim's remaining component
2257: unless the pass rows assigned to that remaining component are also present.
2258: \end{theorem}
2259: 
2260: \begin{proof}
2261: The projected certificate $\mathcal C_J$ is a certificate of the same schema as
2262: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table}, with claim-kind set
2263: $J$ in place of $K$ and with gate field restricted to the finite set $G_J$.
2264: Because $J\subseteq K\subseteq\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)$, Proposition~\ref{prop:claim-kind-dispatch}
2265: assigns exactly the finite gate set $G_J$ to the projected reading.  Applying the
2266: schema-relative exactness theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness},
2267: to $\mathcal C_J$ gives the equivalence: acceptance is exactly satisfaction of
2268: the six finite schema clauses for the retained fields.  Clause A1 is the domain
2269: condition $J\subseteq\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)$ and $\ell\in\mathsf{Lev}$; clause A2
2270: checks the evidence surface $E$; clause A3 checks that each gate in $G_J$ has a
2271: pass record in $R|_{G_J}$ with an allowed pointer; clause A4 checks the retained
2272: promotion boundary $H$; clause A5 checks precisely the retained discovery field
2273: $D_J$; and clause A6 checks the retained command boundary $B$.  These are exactly
2274: the conditions in item~2, so items~1 and~2 are equivalent.
2275: 
2276: Now suppose the row for the mixed claim records only pass rows with gate names in
2277: a proper subset $Q$ of the full assigned gate set.  Choose a nonempty
2278: $J\subseteq K$ with $\neg(G_J\subseteq Q)$ and choose $g\in G_J\setminus Q$.  The
2279: projected verifier for $c_J$ must check $g$ by clause A3, because $g$ is assigned
2280: to the retained component-kind set $J$.  No row for $g$ is present among the
2281: recorded gate names in $Q$, so clause A3 fails.  The verifier reads only the
2282: finite fields consisting of $J$, $\Gamma$, the absent gate name $g$, and the gate
2283: row field $R$; deleting fields not read by this failed check leaves an
2284: inclusion-minimal bad subrecord of the second obstruction type in
2285: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}.  Since the argument
2286: uses only membership in the assigned gate set, it applies uniformly to each of
2287: the seven atomic gate interfaces.  Thus one component's gate passage cannot be
2288: substituted for another component's missing assigned pass rows in a mixed
2289: load-bearing claim.\end{proof}
2290: 
2291: \begin{theorem}[Concrete finite-script projection-contract boundary]
2292: \label{thm:concrete-finite-script-implementation-soundness}
2293: Let $S$ be the submitted support surface containing
2294: \path{review_bundle/certificate_schema.json},
2295: \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json},
2296: \path{review_bundle/submission_interface_map.json},
2297: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json},
2298: \path{source_interface_record.json},
2299: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json}, the bundled pinned source
2300: snapshot, and \path{theorem_inventory.json}.  Let $\mathcal V$ be the four-script
2301: family consisting of \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py},
2302: \path{review_bundle/verify_source_interface_record.py},
2303: \path{review_bundle/verify_primary_claim_inventory.py}, and
2304: \path{review_bundle/extract_theorem_environments.py}.
2305: Assume that the submitted package contains, for each script in $\mathcal V$ used
2306: as command-run evidence, its source path, invocation command, source state,
2307: environment, exit code, and log path.  Then the following schema-level finite
2308: projection contract is proved for these named scripts and no others: on the
2309: finite input projection explicitly parsed by the corresponding script, exit code
2310: $0$ is exact for the script's printed finite predicate, and this is the maximal
2311: record consequence available from the command-run row unless the package pays a
2312: separate positive extension budget in the sense of
2313: Theorems~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} and
2314: \ref{thm:five-axis-upgrade-obstruction}.  Namely,
2315: \path{verify_certificate_records.py} certifies schema-relative certificate-row
2316: coverage and pointer discipline for the current pass-record ledger;
2317: \path{verify_source_interface_record.py} certifies equality of the manuscript
2318: source-interface table with the JSON and pinned-snapshot manifest records;
2319: \path{verify_primary_claim_inventory.py} certifies the primary-claim row shape,
2320: line-span bounds, row-id set, and certificate-surface existence checks; and
2321: \path{extract_theorem_environments.py} certifies syntactic theorem-like
2322: environment extraction and label coverage by \path{theorem_inventory.json}.  This
2323: finite-script projection soundness does not certify mathematical theorem
2324: truth, Lean/BEDC correctness, artifact semantic validity, venue upload,
2325: acceptance, archive availability, verifier-source implementation soundness
2326: beyond the parsed projection, or the operational semantics of Python itself.
2327: Any attempted use of the same exit-code row for one of those stronger readings
2328: is rejected by the finite-record interface with an inclusion-minimal bad
2329: subrecord unless the missing implementation, formal-source, artifact-semantic,
2330: venue, archive, or runtime coordinate and its assigned pass row are submitted.
2331: \end{theorem}
2332: 
2333: \begin{proof}
2334: Each member of $\mathcal V$ is a finite text file included in the submitted
2335: support surface, and the command-run tuple fixes the invoked script, source
2336: state, environment, exit code, and log path.  Inspect the deterministic input
2337: projection named in the statement.
2338: \path{verify_certificate_records.py} reads the certificate schema, the current
2339: pass-record ledger, the submission-interface map, the primary-claim inventory,
2340: the review-bundle manifest, and the selected source surfaces; its accepted output
2341: is obtained only after checking the finite fields used by the schema-relative
2342: certificate predicates: row identifiers, claim-kind sets, evidence levels,
2343: evidence surfaces, human-promotion fields, assigned pass records, discovery
2344: fields, boundary fields, and evidence-pointer existence.  Therefore exit code
2345: $0$ for this script is exactly the accepted side of the finite row-and-pointer
2346: predicate it prints, because every accepted clause is one of those finite
2347: comparisons and an error is appended when such a comparison fails.  This is not
2348: an appeal to a hidden semantics for the script; it is the declared implementation
2349: contract obtained by projecting the command-run record to the finite fields that
2350: the script reads and prints.
2351: 
2352: The same argument applies to the other three scripts after replacing the finite
2353: projection.  The source-interface verifier reads the manuscript table, the JSON
2354: source-interface record, and the pinned snapshot manifest; every accepted clause
2355: is an equality, digest, line-count, or line-range comparison among those finite
2356: records.  The primary-claim verifier reads \path{submission_abstract.tex} and
2357: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json}; every accepted clause is a
2358: finite row-shape, row-id, line-span, nonempty-field, or certificate-pointer test.
2359: The theorem-environment extractor reads \path{main.tex} and
2360: \path{theorem_inventory.json}; every accepted clause is a regular-expression
2361: extraction of labelled theorem-like environments followed by membership of those
2362: labels in the finite inventory.  Thus, in each case, the submitted command-run
2363: row is used only through the script's finite input projection and the finite
2364: predicate displayed by its source and log: if the script exits with code $0$, the
2365: printed predicate holds on the parsed finite projection.
2366: 
2367: No stronger conclusion is used.  The proof never interprets theorem bodies,
2368: checks Lean, evaluates artifacts, contacts an upload system, resolves a public
2369: archive, proves verifier-source implementation soundness beyond the parsed
2370: finite predicates, or gives Python a formal semantics.  Those would require
2371: additional certificate coordinates beyond the source path, command, source state,
2372: environment, exit code, log path, and finite input projection specified here,
2373: and the extension-budget normal form of
2374: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} lists those coordinates.
2375: Conversely, if such a stronger reading were accepted,
2376: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} would force the corresponding
2377: added coordinate to be present and checked.  If it is absent,
2378: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} gives the
2379: inclusion-minimal bad subrecord, and
2380: Theorem~\ref{thm:five-axis-upgrade-obstruction} rules out replacing it by a row
2381: from another interface axis.  Consequently this theorem supplies maximal
2382: schema-level projection soundness only for the named finite-record scripts and
2383: only at the finite predicates just proved; it is not a broad
2384: implementation-correctness theorem for the surrounding pipeline or for Python
2385: execution in general.
2386: \end{proof}
2387: 
2388: 
2389: \begin{corollary}[Finite-script projection instantiation boundary]
2390: \label{cor:finite-script-rgs-instantiation-boundary}
2391: Assume the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:concrete-finite-script-implementation-soundness} and suppose the submitted command-run records for the four named scripts have exit code $0$.  Then the only command-run coordinate instantiated for those scripts is the schema-level finite projection coordinate for the predicates printed by those scripts.  Consequently Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} may use those command-run rows to support schema-relative certificate coverage, source-interface table equality, primary-claim inventory shape, and theorem-environment label coverage, but not mathematical proof truth, formal-source correctness, artifact semantic validity, venue upload, archive availability, broad implementation correctness, or acceptance.
2392: \end{corollary}
2393: 
2394: \begin{proof}
2395: By Theorem~\ref{thm:concrete-finite-script-implementation-soundness}, each exit-code-$0$ command-run record supplies source path, invocation command, source state, environment, exit code, log path, and a finite input projection for one named verifier.  The conclusion of that theorem is precisely soundness for the script's printed finite predicate on that projection, not correctness of the script outside the parsed finite projection.  These are exactly the finite command-run fields demanded by the audit boundary of Theorem~\ref{thm:assigned-gate-soundness-audit-boundary}, restricted to the four named scripts and their printed predicates.  Hence the enlarged coordinate is available only for those finite predicates.
2396: 
2397: Applying Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} with this enlarged coordinate changes only the command-run support available for the listed finite checks.  The finite predicates do not interpret theorem proofs, run Lean or BEDC, evaluate artifacts, contact an upload system, resolve an archive, or read a venue decision.  Such claims would require additional claim kinds, evidence surfaces, pass records, and boundary fields under Theorem~\ref{thm:implementation-soundness-extension-criterion}.  Therefore the instantiated record-gate support has exactly the boundary stated in the corollary.
2398: \end{proof}
2399: \begin{theorem}[Assigned-gate soundness audit boundary]
2400: \label{thm:assigned-gate-soundness-audit-boundary}
2401: Let $P$ be a finite submitted package with pass-record ledger $L$, dispatch
2402: table $\Gamma$, and submitted support surface $S$.  For a load-bearing claim
2403: $c$, write $G(c)=\bigcup_{k\in K(c)}\Gamma(k)$ for the finite set of gates
2404: assigned to its claim-kind set.  The package may use a ledger row for $c$ as a
2405: sound record-gate witness only through the finite predicate
2406: $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)$ of Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}.
2407: Equivalently, the available finite-record strength is exactly the following:
2408: \begin{enumerate}
2409: \item for every $g\in G(c)$ the submitted ledger contains a pass row for $g$,
2410: including the gate name, input surface, decision, evidence pointer, and evidence
2411: level;
2412: \item every evidence pointer named by those pass rows denotes a submitted path,
2413: manifest row, theorem/proposition/table label, uploaded bundle file, or explicitly
2414: declared historical boundary at the evidence level stated in the row; and
2415: \item if the manuscript asserts any stronger proposition that the underlying gate
2416: implementation was independently sound, freshly executed, or semantically correct
2417: beyond the recorded finite schema check, the submitted package also contains a
2418: gate-soundness audit tuple for that stronger proposition: source path, command,
2419: source state, environment, exit code, and log path for the assigned gate; for the
2420: four named finite-record scripts this tuple has exactly the scope proved in